.lua
book.chapter(20, "Diagnostics, recovery, and executable specifications")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A diagnostic has structure"
.pp
Language failures carry a severity, code, message, and source span, with
additional context where available. Session.render combines a diagnostic
with remembered source text to produce a readable display. A machine client
should inspect codes and spans instead of parsing English prose. Messages
can improve while a stable code continues to identify the category of
failure that an editor, test runner, or host application needs to handle.
.lua
book.code("text", [=[M01xx  lexical recognition and malformed literals
M02xx  parsing, binding syntax, and placeholder context
M03xx  expression type checking
M04xx  evaluation and primitive invocation
M05xx  modules and source imports
M06xx  REPL and file directives]=], "Diagnostic families by stage")
.endlua
.pp
Stage information is actionable. A lexical error asks you to inspect the
token spelling, not the function's implementation. A module signature error
asks whether the public contract is satisfied, not whether an arithmetic
branch is correct. A denied primitive may be perfectly well typed yet
unavailable under the embedding's capability policy. Keeping these failures
separate prevents random edits that obscure the original problem.
.sh 2 "Expected failure belongs in data"
.pp
Option and Result are ordinary values, not diagnostics. A parser returning
None or Err lets its caller choose a fallback, annotate an error, or continue
processing other inputs. Core.error deliberately aborts the current
computation with M0407. Use it for a violated invariant when no ordinary
result makes sense, rather than as the default implementation of routine
invalid user input.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[parse_count text := match Int.from_string text with
  | Some n when n >= 0 -> Ok n
  | _ -> Err "expected a non-negative count"
Test.equal "valid input" (parse_count "12") (Ok 12)
Test.equal "malformed input" (parse_count "oops") (Err "expected a non-negative count")
Test.equal "invalid domain" (parse_count "-1") (Err "expected a non-negative count")]=],
    "A validation error remains composable data")
.endlua
.pp
The result type documents the possibility of failure. A consumer must account
for it through matching or a combinator, rather than discovering an evaluator
exception at an arbitrary later point. Rich applications can use an error
ADT instead of String to preserve categories and source positions. Render
human-facing text at the boundary, where the caller knows which filename,
field, or user action supplies the missing context.
.sh 2 "Recognize common runtime codes"
.pp
M0401 is a failed pattern, M0402 integer division by zero, and M0403 a denied
primitive. M0404 covers invalid runtime operations such as rejected bounds.
M0405 reports unsupported comparison, M0406 depth exhaustion, and M0407 an
explicit Core.error. M0408 concerns use before definition. M0409 wraps a
recoverable native callback exception with the primitive's identity. These
codes describe different recovery possibilities and should not all be
flattened into an uninformative "script failed" string.
.lua
book.code("metaml-error:M0407", [=[Core.error "broken invariant"]=],
    "An explicit abort has a diagnostic code")
book.code("metaml-error:M0405", [=[same := (fn x -> x) == (fn x -> x)
same]=], "Function equality is not a generic structural operation")
.endlua
.pp
Diagnostic conversion is not recovery from arbitrary native failure. Invalid
pointers, undefined behavior, process termination, allocation exhaustion, and
infinite computation are outside the promise made by wrapping ordinary host
exceptions. An embedding must design native callbacks carefully and choose
appropriate process-level controls when running untrusted code. The runtime
architecture chapter separates those responsibilities from normal language
error handling.
.sh 2 "Recovery retains useful state, not a transaction log"
.pp
After an evaluation diagnostic, the session resets depth bookkeeping and its
observable value stack so later submissions can proceed without stale
intermediate results. Earlier definitions and effects may remain. This is
useful in an interactive workbench, where one bad expression should not make
the entire session unusable, but it means that retrying a whole file can
repeat earlier work. Recovery tests should check both the error and a later
successful operation in the same session.
.lua
book.algorithm("Test an embedding's recovery contract", {
    "Create a session and define a harmless reusable function.",
    "Submit an expression expected to produce a particular diagnostic code.",
    "Check the returned code and source context.",
    "Submit a later successful call in the same session.",
    "Verify the result and that stale stack/depth state did not leak.",
    "Separately account for any external effects from earlier successful units."
})
.endlua
.pp
Testing only a fresh process after an error misses state-recovery bugs.
Testing only the same process can hide undeclared dependencies between
examples. Use both patterns for their respective purposes: independent
processes establish self-contained programs, while same-session tests
establish recovery and lifetime behavior. The book checker uses fresh
processes because its listings are teaching units, not a shared interactive
transcript with hidden prerequisites.
.sh 2 "Assertions are executable explanations"
.pp
Test.equal compares an actual result with an expected value. Test.check
asserts a boolean property. The small Test module prints deterministic
success lines and aborts on a failed assertion. Meaningful test names explain
the contract: "half-open range" is more informative than "test 3." Put the
actual computation before the expected value consistently so a reader can
audit the example without remembering a local argument convention.
.pp
For errors, assert a stable code rather than an entire rendered message unless
presentation is specifically under test. For approximate numerical behavior,
assert an appropriate bound. For a compiler transformation, assert semantic
preservation and a meaningful shape change. A collection of assertions that
merely repeats the implementation's branches can miss the relationship the
algorithm was meant to preserve. Examples should teach that relationship
as well as demonstrate syntax.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
The canonical code registry is manual/errors.md. Read Session.render, the
runtime's error boundaries, and metalib/test/mod.ml or the Test definition
located through the prelude imports. Add a validator returning an application
error ADT, then render those errors separately. Design a same-session test
that fails once and succeeds afterwards. Explain which state should reset,
which definitions may remain, and why a printed line cannot be rolled back
by clearing an interpreter stack.
